    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 5,
        "sourceEnd": 119,
        "text": "Hi, Leo. Did you finish the reading for sociology? I'm still confused about the two explanations of social change.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "0ed397d42aee613e230a1f8464d8c074d6dfdea1ad37808b622277afd0c3e700",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 125,
        "sourceEnd": 242,
        "text": "Hey, Mia. Yes, I read both. One says technology drives change. The other says social movements do. What do you think?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "64a517cfa4e763ba6cc431af4c66ad38ed4479906ee64b624f49bfdc1679b7c9",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 248,
        "sourceEnd": 427,
        "text": "I think technology is important, but it can't explain everything. For example, the internet changed how we shop, but people also organized online to protest. So maybe both matter.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "7f2f944361d7f0e2c851b64e8c9526c6001fe57048b3eb317f9745e3cdcfd61e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 433,
        "sourceEnd": 658,
        "text": "That's a good point. The first article argues that new tools create new behaviors. It says the smartphone changed family life. But the second article says that's too simple. It argues that people choose how to use technology.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "031a153fceba128cbca8e2a181395bde3952d07da1348494881a4ea7f96032d0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 664,
        "sourceEnd": 841,
        "text": "Right. The second one gives the example of remote work. It became common not just because of laptops, but because workers demanded flexibility. So social pressure played a role.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "243a6b20b9e3c188591c5237828f7945d68beba10a7bcd6c661c6ba8f48cb928",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 847,
        "sourceEnd": 996,
        "text": "Exactly. So the two explanations are not completely separate. One focuses on things, the other on people. I think a better explanation combines them.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "58c573ad34e16483dcf75c288fef51d52537438d3574528c49438500c533fe70",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 1002,
        "sourceEnd": 1136,
        "text": "I agree. But the exam question asks us to compare, not combine. We need to say what each explanation claims and what evidence it uses.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "15fcd3ac280d6773aa1c985c234a1ba790e3a41fd2e5f10cd9ccdfd9befd54ec",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 1142,
        "sourceEnd": 1174,
        "text": "True. Let's make a table. Column",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "334af7e1e5e5ba6a9276134275de53bbe4eb5c40048d98c575b2b938204382be",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "a",
        "speaker": "A",
        "sourceStart": 1178,
        "sourceEnd": 1208,
        "text": "technology explanation. Column",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "dbc40e18523d3460456754cccb73d0bb7c69fa89b6c1ee4f544e6fad14d69a98",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "b",
        "speaker": "B",
        "sourceStart": 1212,
        "sourceEnd": 1273,
        "text": "social movement explanation. Then we can see the differences.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "7d9f7c014e570d6b1767a6f33923e46af3c2d89b028515a73b873b8e87ce7fe8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 1279,
        "sourceEnd": 1513,
        "text": "Good idea. For column A, the key claim is that technology causes change directly. Evidence: the internet and smartphones. For column B, the claim is that collective action causes change. Evidence: labor movements and online campaigns.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "94738dea46f1a97ca4b41bc99721192327e1a00a047bb99f6ce3522d6c5835cc",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 1519,
        "sourceEnd": 1675,
        "text": "But wait, the second article also mentions that technology can help movements. So it's not either-or. The authors might actually agree more than they think.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "b40e9472df2d0a786fe3a9a21954b620ca0e9326a4ca72d299763ea54ffdc1d4",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 1681,
        "sourceEnd": 1828,
        "text": "Maybe. But for the exam, we should present them as separate first, then maybe mention the overlap in a conclusion. That shows deeper understanding.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "3f53d8642d9cec883f3fe38d3e68365b95a29cf830bb74df8e4abfcc3c4d2a3b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 1834,
        "sourceEnd": 1955,
        "text": "Okay. So our answer structure: introduction, explanation A, explanation B, comparison, conclusion. Does that sound right?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "14078cc5b5e96108eb9784402bbdd982bc40dbeb39a2c5988231b220789d554d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 1961,
        "sourceEnd": 2060,
        "text": "Yes. And we must use our own words, not copy sentences. The teacher said paraphrasing is important.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "d5970ce6b83789f8ede175f66c6030ec522bb0f8cc5671df5323f9a39faf4809",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "leo",
        "speaker": "Leo",
        "sourceStart": 2066,
        "sourceEnd": 2207,
        "text": "Got it. Let's start writing the introduction now. We can say that social change is complex and different theories highlight different causes.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "7a2c27eee28a6299c4d242507cce68f51813dad9ef1cc6a7eb217413ae70d184",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "mia",
        "speaker": "Mia",
        "sourceStart": 2213,
        "sourceEnd": 2294,
        "text": "Perfect. I'll write the first part, you write the second, and then we'll compare.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "6b40f453130cef43dedbac6c3c6fb5a0a18c7da27b33ded36ddf84a90576d72c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
